<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Package
  xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10"
  xmlns:mp="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/2014/phone/manifest"
  xmlns:uap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/uap/windows10"
  xmlns:rescap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10/restrictedcapabilities"
  xmlns:desktop6="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/desktop/windows10/6"
  IgnorableNamespaces="uap rescap desktop6">
  <Identity Name="Winora.App" Publisher="CN=Winora" Version="0.3.0.0" />
  <mp:PhoneIdentity PhoneProductId="4370573d-525e-4af2-b3e3-d035695b3753" PhonePublisherId="00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000" />
  <Properties>
    <DisplayName>Winora</DisplayName>
    <PublisherDisplayName>Winora</PublisherDisplayName>
    <Logo>Assets\StoreLogo.png</Logo>
    <!--
      MSIX otherwise redirects this app's HKCU writes into a container view and serves them back on
      read. Winora would then write a value, read its own write, report "applied and verified", and
      leave the real user hive untouched - a verification that lies, which is the one outcome this
      project exists to prevent.

      Measured on 2026-07-31: this element does NOT take effect for an unsigned package registered
      with Add-AppxPackage -Register. Get-AppxPackage then reports SignatureKind None and
      IsDevelopmentMode True, and unvirtualizedResources is a restricted capability the OS does not
      grant to such a package even though it lists it. Nothing at all reached the real hive: with 15
      values in HKCU\...\Run the packaged app read 14 and believed it had disabled the fifteenth,
      while the key it created for itself did not exist outside the package. Every write landed in
      the package write-copy hive at %ProgramData%\Packages\PFN\SID\SystemAppData\Helium\Cache\*.dat,
      which stays mounted for the logon session and survives Remove-AppxPackage plus re-register.

      Resolved on 2026-08-04 by signing the package and installing it with Add-AppxPackage from an
      elevated prompt, verified end to end from outside the app. A dev-mode registration still shows
      the broken behaviour, so never judge a registry domain against one.

      Microsoft documents this element as intended for a narrow set of partner-published titles, so
      it is also incompatible with ordinary Store certification; the MVP ships sideloaded or through
      an enterprise channel, which the specification already fixes.
      Microsoft Learn: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/uwp/schemas/appxpackage/uapmanifestschema/element-desktop6-registrywritevirtualization
    -->
    <desktop6:RegistryWriteVirtualization>disabled</desktop6:RegistryWriteVirtualization>
  </Properties>
  <Dependencies>
    <TargetDeviceFamily Name="Windows.Universal" MinVersion="10.0.22000.0" MaxVersionTested="10.0.26100.0" />
    <TargetDeviceFamily Name="Windows.Desktop" MinVersion="10.0.22000.0" MaxVersionTested="10.0.26100.0" />
  </Dependencies>
  <Resources>
    <Resource Language="x-generate" />
  </Resources>
  <Applications>
    <Application Id="App" Executable="$targetnametoken$.exe" EntryPoint="$targetentrypoint$">
      <uap:VisualElements
        DisplayName="Winora"
        Description="Safe Windows customization"
        BackgroundColor="transparent"
        Square150x150Logo="Assets\Square150x150Logo.png"
        Square44x44Logo="Assets\Square44x44Logo.png">
        <uap:DefaultTile Wide310x150Logo="Assets\Wide310x150Logo.png" />
        <uap:SplashScreen Image="Assets\SplashScreen.png" />
      </uap:VisualElements>
    </Application>
  </Applications>
  <Capabilities>
    <rescap:Capability Name="runFullTrust" />
    <!-- Required by windows.registryWriteVirtualization above. -->
    <rescap:Capability Name="unvirtualizedResources" />
  </Capabilities>
</Package>
